From 992aa14527909cc3c5690607a5e2017ccc19529a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John R Reeves III <johnreevesiii@outlook.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 09:12:53 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 21/22] rec-wasm: fix the ifb guard comment about the insurance
 tick

The comment said a missed page-generation bump was caught by the 1/64
insurance tick. FLY_SMC_INSURANCE_TICK is 0 in this build, so nothing
bounded it. The comment now says so and lists the interpreter steps
the guard covers.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01MGX7KQ5gfrWApgW64pZbQ5
---
 core/rec-wasm/rec_wasm.cpp | 5 ++++-
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/core/rec-wasm/rec_wasm.cpp b/core/rec-wasm/rec_wasm.cpp
index 1fe0e56bc..1dc473dc4 100644
--- a/core/rec-wasm/rec_wasm.cpp
+++ b/core/rec-wasm/rec_wasm.cpp
@@ -865,7 +865,10 @@ u32 EMSCRIPTEN_KEEPALIVE wasm_get_vram_base() {
 // imports. and.b, or.b and xor.b #imm,@(R0,GBR) always decode to ifb, so
 // without this guard their RAM stores left g_fly_page_gen unchanged and a
 // gen-gated dispatch slot could skip the SMC re-hash for the written page
-// until the 1/64 insurance tick. The guard swaps in WriteMem* wrappers that
+// (no insurance tick bounds that in this build: FLY_SMC_INSURANCE_TICK is
+// off, so a missed bump went unnoticed until something else wrote the
+// page). Every single-instruction interpreter step in this file (ref mode,
+// clean dispatch, compile-failed and trap steps) runs under it too. The guard swaps in WriteMem* wrappers that
 // bump the page generation for the duration of one ifb op and restores the
 // previous pointers afterwards (also on an SH4 exception), unless the op
 // itself installed new handlers (an MMU switch), which are kept.
-- 
